Now the wet edge algorithm. Design:

```cpp
void KisWetEdgeOption::apply(KisFixedPaintDeviceSP dab, const KisPaintInformation &info)
{
    if (!isChecked()) return;
    const qreal strength = computeSizeLikeValue(info); // 0..1, sensor-mapped (pressure/speed)
    if (strength <= 0.0) return;

    QRect rc = dab->bounds();
    const int w = rc.width(), h = rc.height();
    if (w < 3 || h < 3) return;

    // edge width in pixels
    const qreal radius = 0.5 * qMin(w, h);
    const qreal edgeWidthPx = qMax<qreal>(1.0, radius * m_edgeWidth / 100.0);

    // 1. extract alpha
    // 2. distance transform (two-pass chamfer) of "inside" mask
    // 3. boost opacity near edges
}
```

Distance transform: alpha > threshold → inside. Chamfer with weights (3,4) for orthogonal/diagonal in fixed point, or use float. Two passes.

Then for each pixel: d = distance; if d < edgeWidthPx: t = 1 - d/edgeWidthPx (1 at edge); newAlpha = alpha + (255 - alpha) * t * strength... but wet edge should darken — in a mask-based dab, "darker" = more pigment = higher opacity. So boost opacity at edges. Also optionally slightly reduce interior to enhance contrast? Keep it simple: boost edges.

